EXT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE - HIGH DESERT TOWN - DAY

A single-street town of sun-bleached boards. A telegraph pole leans over the roof of a squat office. Heat shimmers above the road. The hitching rail holds one horse and nothing else.

INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

A narrow room. A counter with brass grille. A key and sounder on a desk, silent. A wall clock stopped at 4:10. Dust motes hang in a bar of light from the lone window.

CLEMENTINE "CLEM" ODOM, 40s, sits behind the counter in a sleeve garter and green eyeshade. Lean, quiet, careful with her hands. She is sorting tickets into pigeonholes when the bell on the door jingles.

A STRANGER enters. Late thirties, a long gray coat patched at the elbow, a hat pulled low over a face the sun has worked on for years. He carries a saddlebag over one shoulder and does not take it off.

STRANGER
Afternoon.

CLEM
Afternoon.

STRANGER
You the operator?

CLEM
I am.

STRANGER
I need to send a wire.

CLEM
Where to?

STRANGER
Cheyenne.

She slides a form across the counter and a pencil beside it. He looks at the pencil, then at her, and picks it up with his left hand. Clem notes this and says nothing.

CLEM
Fifteen words for a quarter. Each one after is two cents.

STRANGER
I'll keep it short.

He writes slowly. Clem returns to her pigeonholes, but watches him sidelong. The saddlebag shifts on his shoulder with a faint metallic clink.

CLEM
That heavy?

STRANGER
Some.

CLEM
You can set it down.

STRANGER
I'm fine.

He finishes, pushes the form back. She reads it, lips barely moving.

CLEM
"Arrived. Will wait. Send word by Tuesday." No name.

STRANGER
No name.

CLEM
They'll want to know who it's from.

STRANGER
They'll know.

A silence. The sounder on the desk begins to chatter, a rapid stutter of clicks. Clem turns, listens, takes up a pencil, and writes on a pad as the code runs. Her face changes by degrees. The Stranger watches her pencil.

CLEM (CONT'D)
(without turning)
You want to tell me your name, mister?

STRANGER
Why?

CLEM
Because this just came over the line for a man matching your description.

She tears off the sheet, holds it up between two fingers. He doesn't take it.

STRANGER
What's it say?

CLEM
It says "Deputy Marcus Reyes, lawman, last seen heading north with a stolen payroll."

The Stranger's right hand drifts toward his coat. Clem's drifts under the counter.

STRANGER
Ma'am.

CLEM
Marcus.

He stops.

MARCUS
Put your hand where I can see it.

CLEM
I will when you've shown me yours.

He slowly lifts both hands away from his coat. The saddlebag slides to the floor with a heavy chunk.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
That's not the payroll.

CLEM
Then what is it?

MARCUS
Evidence.

CLEM
Of what?

MARCUS
Of who took it.

Clem's hand comes up from beneath the counter, empty. She lays the sheet down on the grille.

CLEM
Then you'd best read the rest of this.

He steps up, reads. His lips tighten. The sounder chatters again.

VOICE ON THE WIRE (V.O.)
(telegraph operator, Cheyenne; read aloud by Clem)
Marshal's office confirms. Reyes is clean. Hold him. Dangerous man trails.

Marcus lifts his eyes from the page and meets Clem's. Outside, at the end of the street, the clatter of hooves begins and grows.

CLEM
That'd be your dangerous man.

MARCUS
Lock the door.

She does. He pulls the shade. In the dim, they stand a pace apart, listening, while the hoofbeats come on and the sounder, on its desk, goes quiet.
